# @NAME@

Editable JUCE VST3 **@DSP@** starter generated by Fourier (`IS_SYNTH=@SYNTH@`).
All variants have mono/stereo output, a smoothed −60..+12 dB output gain,
JUCE's generic accessible parameter editor and APVTS parameter state.
This is small, readable teaching DSP, **not a production processor or a model of
any commercial plug-in**. No proprietary code/assets are supplied.

## DSP choices

Only the selected variant is active; the other branches in `StarterDSP.h` compile
out. Edit `PluginProcessor.cpp`, `.h`, `StarterDSP.h` and (for subtractive synthesis)
`SubtractiveVoice.h` using normal file tools; do not regenerate over edited DSP.

| Variant | Algorithm and controls | Important limitations |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `gain` | Transparent effect; 20 ms linear-amplitude gain smoothing | No limiter or loudness normalization |
| `filter` | One RBJ biquad/channel; low-pass, high-pass or peak EQ; frequency, Q, EQ dB | No crossover bank; frequency/Q/gain smooth over 20 ms, mode switches do not; extreme automation may produce transients |
| `compressor` | Stereo-linked sample-peak gain computer; threshold, ratio, quadratic soft knee; attack/release one-pole smoothing of linear gain | No RMS detector, lookahead, sidechain bus, auto makeup or oversampling; controls are not calibrated response times of a vendor model |
| `saturation` | `tanh(drive*x)/drive` with dry/wet and smoothed drive | No oversampling; aliasing at high drive; output decreases as drive rises, no automatic makeup |
| `delay` | Independent channels, preallocated two-second ring, fractional linear read, feedback and dry/wet | Time changes use a 20 ms moving read (pitch changes), not crossfades; no tempo sync; reported tail is conservative to −60 dB at maximum delay/feedback |
| `sine` | Eight sine voices, velocity, fixed ADSR, JUCE sustain and voice stealing | No pitch bend/MPE/modulation |
| `subtractive` | Eight voices; sine/polyBLEP saw/square, one-pole low-pass, adjustable ADSR, velocity, sustain, ±2-semitone pitch bend, CC1 vibrato | No resonance, modulation matrix, MPE or multi-oscillator unison; polyBLEP reduces but does not eliminate aliasing; cutoff/wave/ADSR changes are block-updated and can click |

Effects report zero algorithmic latency. Dry/wet paths introduce no hidden
latency compensation; delay is the intended effect. Instrument tails are 0.1 s
for sine and a conservative 3 s for subtractive. MIDI is dispatched at sample
positions (synth subdivision size one). Gain is post-effect/post-synthesis.

## Build

Use the extension's `fourier_juce` tool with action `build`, this project directory,
and `juce_path` pointing to your local JUCE source checkout. Or run:

```sh
cmake -S . -B build -DFOURIER_JUCE_PATH=/path/to/JUCE -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build --config Release --target @NAME@_VST3 --parallel 2
```

Requires CMake 3.22+, a C++17 compiler, and JUCE (validated with JUCE 8).
On Windows use a VS developer shell with Ninja or a supported Visual Studio
generator. Linux needs JUCE's native audio/GUI development libraries; macOS
needs Xcode command-line tools. No SDK downloads or system installation occur.

The local bundle is `build/@NAME@_artefacts/Release/VST3/@NAME@.vst3`.
Use `fourier_inspect`, then `fourier_plan`/`fourier_run` with preset `effect` or
`generator`. Native numeric parameters are normalized **0..1**, not physical
Hz/dB; inspect exact keys/text/readbacks. Use plan `automation` for timed changes
and `save_state`/`state_file` for identity/settings-bound Fourier snapshots.
`fourier_match` pairs a reference plan with this candidate and can search a
small explicit parameter grid with held-out cases. These are behavioral
comparisons, not proof of recovered algorithms or perceptual equivalence.

Builds/loading execute native code; use only trusted source/SDKs. Full build logs
are retained. Keep audio-thread code allocation-, lock-, I/O- and network-free.
Preserve parameter IDs, state compatibility, bus layouts, sample-rate handling,
reset/tail behavior and MIDI timing. APVTS stores parameters, not voices, delay
memory or a sample-exact resumable processing session. Test your edited DSP in
an actual DAW before release; Fourier does not host a live UI or playback.

Assign your own company, bundle ID and unique four-character IDs in
`CMakeLists.txt` before distributing. Review your JUCE version's licence and
licensing terms; generation grants no JUCE licence. No signing, notarization,
installer, custom GUI or other plug-in formats are supplied. Fourier's manifest
`fourier-project.json` records the target name/kind/DSP; update its name too if
you rename the CMake target.
